{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789784301,"nonce":"LaxVbki+lVqrKnPqUXlBYKpTp3s=","title":"bitwise operators bind below the comparisons in both parsers","message":"`n \u0026 0x8000 != 0` parsed as `n \u0026 (0x8000 != 0)` — C's ladder, not Haxe's,\nin the RD parser's precedence table (rd/expr.rs: `\u0026` 7, `==` 8) and in the\nlegacy chain (haxe_parser_expr.rs: bitwise_or → xor → and → equality). In\nHaxe `\u0026 | ^` share one level ABOVE the comparisons, `...` sits below them,\nand `%` above `*`. `Input.readInt16`'s sign fix (`if (n \u0026 0x8000 != 0)\nreturn n - 0x10000`) fired for every value, so `BytesInput.readInt16` of 7\nwas -65529; the idiom is everywhere (`flags \u0026 MASK != 0`).\n\nFix: the RD table follows the Haxe ladder; the legacy chain parses\n`| ^ \u0026` at one level between the relational operators and the shifts, with\n`|`/`\u0026` refusing the first character of `||`/`\u0026\u0026`.\n\nFixture: haxe-conformance/regressions/OperatorPrecedence.hx.","files":null}]}